<<<
but that relation is adequately given by greater &c. part, & only confused by the dragging in of the comparison of ratios expressed by p.
===
but that relation is adequately given by *greater* &c. *part*, & only confused by the dragging in of the comparison of ratios expressed by *p.*
>>>

<<<
 It is a clumsy blunder to use words like greater & largest with p. when the comparison is between the parts of one whole & not between the ratios borne by parts of different wholes to their respective wholes.
===
 It is a clumsy blunder to use words like *greater* & *largest* with *p.* when the comparison is between the parts of one whole & not between the ratios borne by parts of different wholes to their respective wholes.
>>>

<<<
 To give contrasted examples of the wrong & the right: We passed the greater proportion of our candidates is wrong; read part; We hope to pass a greater proportion of our candidates next year is right.
===
 To give contrasted examples of the wrong & the right: *We passed the greater proportion of our candidates* is wrong; read *part*; *We hope to pass a greater proportion of our candidates next year* is right.
>>>

